---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
// Landing page for offline field data collection. Facts are checked
// against the in-portal help page "Data collection" and the public
// demo. The screenshots are the installed field app on an iPhone.
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import fieldDownload from '../assets/shots/field-offline-download.webp';
import fieldCollect from '../assets/shots/field-offline-collect.webp';
import fieldSync from '../assets/shots/field-offline-sync.webp';
import { DEMO_URL } from '../lib/site';

const steps = [
  {
    when: 'Before leaving',
    what: 'Download an area',
    src: fieldDownload,
    alt: 'The field app on a phone saying the deployment is ready for offline, with 3 layers, 54 features, 1 form and 2 pick lists cached.',
  },
  {
    when: 'In the field',
    what: 'Collect without signal',
    src: fieldCollect,
    alt: 'A road and trail issue report form filled in on a phone in airplane mode, with a large Submit button at the bottom.',
  },
  {
    when: 'Back in range',
    what: 'Edits sync on their own',
    src: fieldSync,
    alt: 'The field map back online, with a Sync 1 button in the header for the edit made offline.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Offline field data collection app, open source | GratisGIS"
  description="Collect GIS data offline on any phone: download an area, capture points, photos and forms without signal, sync later. Open source, no per-user licenses."
  path="/field-data-collection/"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Offline field data collection', path: '/field-data-collection/' }]}
>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Field app</p>
    <h1>Offline field data collection without per-user licenses</h1>
    <p>
      GratisGIS includes a field app for collecting GIS data on a phone or tablet, with or without
      signal. It runs in the phone’s browser and can be added to the home screen, so there is
      nothing to install from an app store. Every collector signs in with their own account, and
      adding collectors costs nothing.
    </p>

    <ol class="steps steps-shots">
      {
        steps.map((step) => (
          <li>
            <Picture
              src={step.src}
              formats={['avif', 'webp']}
              fallbackFormat="webp"
              widths={[240, 360, 480]}
              sizes="(min-width: 900px) 240px, 31vw"
              alt={step.alt}
              class="phone"
            />
            <span class="when">{step.when}</span>
            <strong>{step.what}</strong>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>

    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Set up a deployment at your desk.</strong> Pick a map, mark which layers the crew
        can edit, and attach a form to each one if you want something other than the default.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hand it to the crew.</strong> Send a link or show the QR code. They open it on
        their own phone and sign in.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Download before leaving.</strong> One tap saves the layers, forms, pick lists and
        the map for the work area onto the phone.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Collect all day.</strong> Add points, lines and areas, fill in the form and attach
        photos. Edits wait in a queue on the phone while there is no signal.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sync when back in range.</strong> Queued edits upload to the data layer, where
        maps and dashboards pick them up right away.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Built for use outdoors</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Large text and buttons, in light and dark mode, using the phone’s own system font.</li>
      <li>
        The collect, locate and layers buttons sit together within reach of one thumb. A setting
        moves them to the left for left-handed use.
      </li>
      <li>A GPS accuracy readout, so the crew knows how good a position is before saving it.</li>
      <li>A count of edits waiting to sync, always visible in the header.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Forms</h2>
    <p>
      Each editable layer gets a form built from its fields, or you can design your own in the
      form builder with required questions, pick lists and photos. The form builder imports
      XLSForm, so existing surveys do not have to be retyped.
    </p>

    <h2>Your data stays yours</h2>
    <p>
      Collected features go straight into PostgreSQL with PostGIS on your own server. They are
      readable in <a href="/qgis/">QGIS</a> and through standard OGC APIs the moment they sync.
    </p>

    <h2>What it is not</h2>
    <p>
      The field app is a web app, not a native iOS or Android app, and it is not a drop-in
      replacement for ArcGIS Field Maps or Survey123. If you depend on external GNSS receiver
      integration or Survey123’s advanced form logic, test your workflow on the demo first. The{' '}
      <a href="/arcgis-online-alternative/">comparison with ArcGIS Online</a> covers the rest.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href={`${DEMO_URL}/field`}>Try the field app on your phone</a>
      <a class="btn btn-secondary btn-lg" href="/install/">Read the install guide</a>
    </div>
    <p class="fineprint">
      The demo’s sign-in page lists shared test accounts. Everything on it resets each night.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
